examples.question_text:
Find the midpoint of the interval $AB$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{intervalblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[xstep=7mm,ystep=7mm,color=gridgrey,thin] (-3,-1) grid (5,5);
\draw[<->,black] (-3.4,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-1.4)--(0,5.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw[black] (\v,-0.08)--(\v,0.08);\path (\v,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} {\draw[black] (-0.08,\v)--(0.08,\v);\path (0,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[thick,intervalblue] (-2,1)--(4,5);
\fill[black] (-2,1) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (4,5) circle (2pt);
\path (-2,1) node[above left] {$A$};
\path (4,5) node[above right] {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Read the endpoints: $A(-2,1)$ and $B(4,5)$. Average the corresponding coordinates.
$\begin{aligned}\overline{x}&=\frac{-2+4}{2}\\&=1\end{aligned}$
$\begin{aligned}\overline{y}&=\frac{1+5}{2}\\&=3\end{aligned}$
$M=(1,3)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{intervalblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{midpointred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[<->,black] (-3,0)--(5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.5)--(0,5.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,intervalblue] (-2,1)--(4,5);
\fill[black] (-2,1) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (4,5) circle (2pt);
\fill[midpointred] (1,3) circle (2.5pt);
\path (-2,1) node[above left] {$A(-2,1)$};
\path (4,5) node[above] {$B(4,5)$};
\path (1,3) node[below right] {$M(1,3)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both endpoints have $y=2$, so the midpoint also has $y=2$.
$\begin{aligned}\overline{x}&=\frac{-5+4}{2}\\&=-0.5\end{aligned}$
$M=(-0.5,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{intervalblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{midpointred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[<->,black] (-5.8,0)--(4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->,black] (0,-0.5)--(0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,intervalblue] (-5,2)--(4,2);
\fill[black] (-5,2) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (4,2) circle (2pt);
\fill[midpointred] (-0.5,2) circle (2.5pt);
\path (-5,2) node[above] {$A(-5,2)$};
\path (4,2) node[above] {$B(4,2)$};
\path (-0.5,2) node[below left] {$M(-0.5,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both endpoints have $x=2$, so the midpoint also has $x=2$.
$\begin{aligned}\overline{y}&=\frac{-4+3}{2}\\&=-0.5\end{aligned}$
$M=(2,-0.5)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{intervalblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{midpointred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[->,black] (-0.5,0)--(4.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-4.8)--(0,3.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,intervalblue] (2,-4)--(2,3);
\fill[black] (2,-4) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (2,3) circle (2pt);
\fill[midpointred] (2,-0.5) circle (2.5pt);
\path (2,-4) node[right] {$P(2,-4)$};
\path (2,3) node[right] {$Q(2,3)$};
\path (2,-0.5) node[below right] {$M(2,-0.5)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
